# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Word/Office lock files — these appear in class folders and must never be committed
~$*

# Ignored from the first commit, which is the only thing that makes an ignore rule
# reliable. Put anything here you want kept out of a fork you publish — including your
# filled-in copies of the Context/ pack files. See the note at the bottom of this file.
Context/private/

# Never commit anything from the class side. If you ever put a git repo near student
# work, these are the lines that matter — and do not add a remote. There is a
# deny-by-default template for the class side in
# structure/class-side-gitignore-template.txt; prefer it to these four lines.
_reports/
students/
*.docx
*.doc

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE YOU PUSH A FORK
#
# The Context/ pack files — school-profile.md, teaching-beliefs.md, writing-style.md and
# anything you save into Context/curriculum/ — are TRACKED in this repo. Adding them to
# this file later does NOT untrack them: .gitignore only affects files git is not already
# following. So if you fork, fill them with your real school detail and push, you publish
# them, and no amount of editing this file afterwards changes that.
#
# For most people that is fine — there is no student data in those files and much of it is
# worth sharing. If it is not fine for you, do one of these BEFORE your first push:
#
#   * Keep your filled-in versions in Context/private/ (ignored above), or
#   * untrack them:
#       git rm --cached Context/school-profile.md Context/teaching-beliefs.md Context/writing-style.md
#       printf '%s\n' Context/school-profile.md Context/teaching-beliefs.md Context/writing-style.md >> .gitignore
#     then confirm with: git status --short
#   * or simply never add a remote. Local git still gives you the undo and the tripwire
#     against cloud-sync clobbers, which is most of why you wanted it.
